<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06YA">Remark <span data-tag="06YA">7.1.1.5</span>.</a> Let $n \geq -2$ be an integer and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which is locally $n$-truncated (Definition <a data-tag="0584" href="/tag/0584">4.7.6.1</a>). Then, for every simplicial set $K$, the restriction map $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{sk}_{n+1}(K), \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is fully faithful (see Corollary <a data-tag="06Y8" href="/tag/06Y8">4.8.8.21</a>). It follows that an object $X$ is a limit (or colimit) of a diagram $u: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if and only if it is a limit (or colimit) of the restriction $u|_{ \operatorname{sk}_{n+1}(K) }$. </p>
